{:tier :glm-5.2, :verse 3, :comments ["<b>And the waters diminished at the end of one hundred and fifty days.</b> That is on the first of Sivan. 'And the ark rested in the seventh month' — in Sivan." "<b>On the tenth, etc.</b> This is Av, which is the tenth to Marcheshvan, in which the rains began to fall, for one may not interpret [it as] the tenth to the cessation, which is Elul, for Rashi explained: 'And it came to pass at the end of forty days, and Noah opened, etc.' — at the end of forty days from when the tops of the mountains were seen. If so, according to his interpretation and according to the verses, there are sixty days from when the tops of the mountains were seen until the face of the earth dried. And if I were to say that they were not seen until Elul, when you count eight days thereafter you will find them ending on the first of Marcheshvan. And likewise: 'In the first, on the first of the month, [the waters] were seen, etc.' — the waters dried; from here [supports] one who holds that it is Marcheshvan called 'first.' Rather, certainly the tenth is Av, and on its first the tops of the mountains were seen, and when you count sixty days you will find them ending on the first of Tishrei, which is 'first' according to Rabbi Eliezer. From here [we learn] that 'the seventh' is Sivan and 'the tenth' is Av. If so, from the cessation of intensification, which is the first of Sivan, until the tops of the mountains were seen, which is the first of Av, there are sixty days, and fifteen cubits above the mountains were the waters — meaning that in sixty days the waters diminished fifteen cubits, which is one cubit per four days. If so, in sixteen days they diminished four cubits; in sixteen days from when they began to diminish, [the ark] rested on the mountains. If so, you will find that it was submerged in the water eleven cubits. All this Rashi explained.\n\nBut there is a difficulty with what we explained, that 'at the end of forty days, and Noah opened' — that is, from when the tops of the mountains were seen — for if so, the dove which he sent seven days after those forty days: why did it not find rest? But could it not have rested on the mountains? Therefore Rashi explained: 'And it came to pass at the end of forty days' — from when the waters began to diminish. And according to my humble opinion, it must be that there were trees on the mountains that had already been seen, and so the event was: 'And the waters diminished at the end of one hundred and fifty days' — and that is the first of Sivan. 'And the ark rested in the seventh month' — on the seventeenth of Sivan. And forty days after the waters began to diminish — 'Noah opened the window of the ark' — that is the tenth of Tammuz. And he sent the raven immediately, and it did not perform its errand. And he waited seven days and sent the dove, and it was the seventeenth of Tammuz, and the dove did not find rest. And I say that this is also an allusion to the beginning of the destruction, which was on the seventeenth of Tammuz, when the city was breached, and Israel found no rest on it — for they are called 'dove.' And he waited yet another seven days, and it found an olive, as it is written: 'and behold, an olive leaf plucked in her mouth' — and on that day the tops of the trees must have been seen. And he waited yet another seven days and sent again, and it is the month of Av, which is the tenth to the descent — 'on the first of the month.' 'And it did not return to him anymore,' for it found rest on the tops of the mountains that were seen on that day. Thus you have it: sixty days from the cessation of intensification until the tops of the mountains were seen, just as according to Rashi's interpretation — from the first of Av, when they were seen, until the first of Tishrei, there are sixty days between them; the waters dried, as it is written: 'In the first, on the first of the month, the waters dried, etc.' Reckon and you will find that from when the tops of the mountains were seen until the waters dried there are sixty days, just as according to Rashi. And so I found in Seder Olam as I explained.\n\nHowever, this is not its language.\n\nBut it is difficult for me: since it must be that there were trees on the mountains and they were seen before the mountains were seen, as Rashi explained — why did he not write the day of the seeing of the trees, just as he wrote the day of the seeing of the mountains? And if you will say that no tree remained at all except an olive, as it is written: 'And he blotted out, etc.' — for this is the implication according to Rashi's explanation, for regarding 'and he planted a vineyard,' when Noah entered the ark he brought with him bundles of vine-branches, meaning that no trees remained, and because of seeing one tree he did not write the day of its seeing. Even so, the question remains: why did an olive remain more than other trees? And if you say that it remained by merit, because they are destined to make oil from its fruit for the lamp — also a vineyard, they are destined to make libations from its fruit. Yet one may say that a vineyard had a remedy through bringing vine-branches into the ark, but for an olive Noah could not make this remedy, for it would dry out in the year of the flood more than a vine; therefore an olive remained and not a vineyard."], :book :genesis, :voice :daat-zkenim, :lang :en, :chapter 8, :notes {}, :source-witnesses [{"versionTitle" "Sefaria community Hebrew (PD)", "language" "he"}], :timestamp "2026-07-30T13:05:25.501583613Z", :translated-from :he, :model "glm-5.2"}